; Nevarro LLC — 2026
;
; Built from CSV imports (see ../csv/ and ../rules/):
;   csv/chase-cc-2026.csv   + rules/chase-cc.csv.rules
;   csv/bluevine-2026.csv   + rules/bluevine.csv.rules
;   csv/fidelity-2026.csv   + rules/fidelity.csv.rules
;
; equity:transfers is a clearing account for Chase Credit Card payments —
; both the paying account's CSV and Chase's own CSV record their half of
; the same real-world transfer independently, so each side posts against
; this account rather than guessing which account paid. It should net to
; $0.00 once all three CSVs are fully imported for a period; if it doesn't,
; that means one side of some transfer is missing.

account assets:bluevine
account assets:fidelity
account liabilities:chase-cc
account equity:opening-balances
account equity:member-capital
account equity:transfers
account income:interest
account income:credits-and-adjustments
account expenses:hosting:hetzner
account expenses:domains:namecheap
account expenses:comms:sendgrid
account expenses:comms:migadu
account expenses:comms:google-workspace
account expenses:devtools:github
account expenses:backup:backblaze
account expenses:monitoring:uptimerobot
account expenses:fees:government
account expenses:fees:foreign-transaction

; Opening balances as of 2026-01-01, derived from actual bank data rather
; than by-month/2025-12.md. That report's Bluevine figure ($447.38) turns
; out to be the balance *after* the 2026-01-05 Chase autopay had already
; posted, not the true 2025-12-31 balance — Bluevine's own running-balance
; column shows $525.85 held continuously from 2025-12-04 (last txn before
; the 2026-01-05 one) through year-end. Chase CC's true 2026-01-01 balance
; ($78.47, vs. the report's $103.33) is likewise back-derived: it's the
; only starting balance consistent with the real January charges ($222.12)
; and payment ($78.47) reconciling to the reported 2026-01-31 balance
; ($222.12). This ~1-statement-cycle labeling lag in the old monthly
; reports is exactly the kind of gap this conversion is meant to catch.
